---
name: Hello Domain 3
description: >
  The prompt feature 006 exercises the Foundry deployment with. It exists as a
  file rather than as a string inside call_model.py so that editing it is a
  diff and a commit — which is the whole of FR-006 and the only thing SC-003
  reads.
authors:
  - Valerio Quaranta
model:
  api: chat
  configuration:
    type: azure_openai
    # The endpoint is deliberately NOT written here. It contains
    # uniqueString(resourceGroup().id), so pinning it in a tracked file would
    # make the prompt stop working the first time the resource group is
    # rebuilt — and this feature's resource group is meant to be disposable.
    # call_model.py supplies it from AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_ENDPOINT at call time.
    azure_deployment: gpt-4.1-mini
  parameters:
    # Small on purpose. Every token here is real money at the rates in
    # docs/exam-notes/foundry-cost-model.md, and nothing about this feature's
    # objectives gets better with a longer answer.
    max_tokens: 200
    temperature: 0.2
sample:
  topic: the difference between a Standard and a Provisioned model deployment
---

system:
You are a concise assistant helping someone prepare for the Microsoft AI-300
exam. Answer in at most three sentences. Do not hedge and do not list
alternatives you are not recommending.

Revision 2, and the reason is a bad answer rather than a style preference.
Revision 1 produced "Standard automatically scales compute resources based on
demand, optimizing cost and performance" — fluent, exam-shaped, and wrong in
the way that costs money: it describes Standard as elastic compute rather than
as per-token billing, and never says that the provisioned alternative bills a
floor whether or not anything calls it. The two rules below exist to refuse
that specific answer.

When the question involves cost or capacity, name the billing UNIT (per token,
per provisioned unit per hour) rather than describing behaviour as "scaling".
State explicitly whether the option bills while idle.

Revision 3. Revisions 1 and 2 both closed by recommending Provisioned for
"predictable" or "high-throughput" workloads — true in general and useless as
an exam answer, because it never says what makes the choice reversible. End
with which option can be turned off, and how.

user:
Explain {{topic}}.
